      "claim": "GAZT, a polar glucuronide nucleoside, showed poor CSF/plasma ratio (0.04) in monkeys; however, its ionizable glucuronic acid and small nucleoside scaffold are less transferable to the neutral ethylidene-glucose etoposide-like query, so this negative does not override the positive glycoside evidence.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C14"],
      "claim": "Stepholidine showed good brain penetration with an AUC brain:plasma ratio of ~0.7 by microdialysis, supporting that polycyclic natural products with polar functionality can achieve good CNS exposure.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "Predicted pass: the strongest transferable evidence (C04, C07) directly shows glycosylated polyphenols and neutral glycosides reaching brain tissue in vivo, and the query shares the glycoside and polyphenolic features of these positive anchors. The single negative glucuronide (C08) is less transferable due to its acidic sugar, so meaningful CNS access is supported.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct etoposide-specific brain or CSF pharmacokinetic record was retrieved.",
    "Quantitative unbound brain exposure and efflux-transporter status for the query are not directly evidenced.",
    "Positive glycoside anchors may partly reflect aglycone formation after oral administration, so intact-glycoside brain exposure is uncertain."
